00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:04.230 align:start position:0%
ireland's decision to call a temporary  halt to the use of the oxford  astrazeneca vaccine  could have far-reaching consequences the  country's immunization authority pointed  to a safety alert raised this week  by the norwegian medicines agency after  four healthcare workers  suffered serious blood clotting events  within 14 days  of receiving the astrazeneca jab denmark  and iceland have raised similar concerns  with the ripple effect extending to  bulgaria and as far afield as  thailand the irish move was described  as precautionary we will continue to  monitor the situation and if we can be  satisfied that these events are  coincidental  and not caused by this vaccine we will  reassess the situation  the uk-based astrazeneca has defended  its vaccine  saying that safety data collected with  17 million doses administered so  far shows no evidence of increased risk  of pulmonary embolisms or thrombosis  pending further investigation the  european medicines agency  says the benefits of the vaccine  outweigh the risks  and the world health organization says  its rollout should continue  it really doesn't mean that it is  potentially unsafe  uh it has been tested widely there have  been no reported problems  uh beyond what you would normally expect  with a vaccine  with any vaccine and really the the  occurrence of  these thrombotic events these blood  clots uh is no greater in the vaccinated  population  than it is in the unvaccinated  population  so quite why regulators in these  countries have taken their decision  beyond being extremely cautious  is not really clear inevitably now  concerns will linger the oxford  astrazeneca vaccine has been beset by  problems including political disputes  and delivery delays  now add safety concerns it's important  to note that no one  is alleging a conclusive link between  the vaccine  and these blood clotting events but the  longer that doubt is allowed to persist  the worse the effect on trust and  therefore  the overall vaccine rollout jonah al  jazeera london
 

